TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Final Report (Part 2): Space Mission Design","overview":"Develop a preliminary space-mission design that benefits Australia and report the mission concept and its space-segment elements. Use mission-design software of your choice, supported by hand calculations if useful, and justify the proposed design."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Introduction: mission concept and rationale. Include the following required information: What is the spacecraft's primary mission? Why is the mission needed? How many spacecraft are used and why? Who are the users (operator and customers)?","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Addresses all requirements to a high standard. Detailed descriptions and discussions are included. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Addresses some requirements. Descriptions may feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Many or all requirements are not addressed. Poor descriptions or lack of appropriate referencing."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Main body: mission design. Present a summary of the mission design using software and/or hand calculations as appropriate. Include relevant screenshots and software files in the main body of the report and/or appendix. Include the following information: Where will it launch from, and using which launch vehicle? Why did you choose this option? What type of orbit will you use? What are its characteristics and orbital elements? Will you need to perform any orbital maneuvers? Which ground control facilities will you use? How often will your spacecraft be able to communicate with ground control? How long will the mission last, and what are your plans for EOL?","weight":60,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Demonstrates high level understanding of the design procedure. Design clearly addresses mission and course concepts. Major requirements are all addressed and are well supported with quality evidence (e.g. software screenshots, software files, hand calculations, references from literature). Content is comprehensive, accurate, and persuasive."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Demonstrates adequate understanding of the design procedure. Design may be inconsistent with regards to purpose and clarity of thought or feature errors. Major requirements are addressed but may lack some detail or are not well supported with evidence. Content may not be comprehensive and/or persuasive."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Demonstrates minimal understanding of the design procedure. Major requirements are not addressed and/or lack evidence. Content is incomplete or not persuasive."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Conclusion","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Detailed and appropriate conclusions reached from the evidence provided in the report."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Some conclusions are reached but they may be generic or lack specific detail."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful conclusions reached."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical engineering report structure. The report is easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Executive summary (or abstract) provides a concise overview of the report. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[],"technical_issues":[{"where":"Orbit","issue":"Inclination of 87.79 deg is incorrect for a 600 km sun-synchronous orbit (about 97.8 deg retrograde). This contradicts the retrograde claim and the student's own Figure 3."},{"where":"Launch","issue":"Kourou latitude is stated as 45.05 deg N; it is about 5.2 deg N, and the equatorial-launch argument depends on it."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"RAAN of 27.33 deg is said to indicate the launch facility location. RAAN is set by launch time and is not a site indicator; the value is unjustified."},{"where":"Ground Control","issue":"Text states that polar latitudes maximise pass frequency, then selects Tasmania (about 43 deg S) without quantitative access comparison."},{"where":"Ground Control","issue":"Text says average contact is almost twenty minutes, but the table mean of 580.786 s is about 9.7 minutes. The text also refers to fifteen accesses while listing fourteen with a duplicated label 13."},{"where":"Ground Control","issue":"Text says the satellite is constantly in sunlight on the descending track; a 600 km SSO has eclipse seasons."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","issue":"Five-year lifetime and EOL choice are asserted without decay or drag analysis. The drag sail sentence is truncated and the final EOL strategy is not stated."},{"where":"Launch","issue":"Piggyback on a rideshare is proposed, but the satellite is then assigned to Vega E, with an unresolved schedule against the 2024 first-launch claim and the 2025 STK epoch."},{"where":"Bibliography","issue":"Wikipedia is cited as a source despite the brief, and table citations [49]-[74] are missing from the list."}],"overall":"The report covers most required segments with STK evidence, but a fundamental orbit error (inclination), wrong launch-site latitude, unsupported RAAN rationale, and missing perturbation calculations weaken the design. Access statistics are misreported, EOL is unfinished, and citations and presentation are inconsistent.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Adequate","mark":6.0,"scale":10.0,"points":6.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"Primary mission, rationale and single-spacecraft choice are clearly argued with some sources. Users are only implied: the operator and customers are not explicitly identified in the introduction, and the referencing is URL-only. Detail is good, but the users requirement is weakly covered.","evidence":[{"where":"Introduction","quote":"Coral reefs, specifically the Great Barrier Reef, would immensely benefit from dedicated satellite monitoring."},{"where":"Introduction","quote":"Thus, only one satellite is required."},{"where":"Introduction","quote":"would allow marine and conservation scientists to more accurately predict"}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Adequate","mark":4.0,"scale":10.0,"points":24.0,"max":60.0,"reasoning":"Most required items are addressed and STK access data and ground tracks are shown. However, the inclination of 87.79 deg is wrong for a 600 km sun-synchronous orbit (about 97.8 deg, as the student's own plot shows), contradicting the retrograde claim. Kourou's latitude is wrong, the RAAN rationale is incorrect, and promised perturbation calculations are absent. Launch vehicle choice is inconsistent.","evidence":[{"where":"Orbit","quote":"Specifically, an altitude of 600km requires a mean inclination of"},{"where":"Orbit","quote":"The orbit is near-polar and retrograde, meaning the satellite will pass over almost the entire Earth"},{"where":"Launch","quote":"it is situated very close to the equator, with a latitude of"},{"where":"Orbit","quote":"The initial value indicates the location of the launch facility."},{"where":"Executive Summary","quote":"including Systems Tool Kit (STK) simulations, perturbation calculations, launch mission alternatives evaluation"}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":5.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The conclusion summarises the segments and identifies the Vega E launch risk. It largely restates the design rather than drawing specific conclusions from the evidence, and it does not reflect the errors in orbit and access results.","evidence":[{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"Thus, launch may need to be adjusted if the rocket is still not constructed by the proposed date."},{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"A polar sun-synchronous orbit allows for consistent illumination of the oceans"}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Adequate","mark":4.0,"scale":10.0,"points":8.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"Headings, a contents list and a numbered executive summary give structure. Sentences are garbled, and tables and figures are largely unnumbered or uncaptioned. Citations [49]-[74] are not in the bibliography, Wikipedia is cited despite the brief, and some references are truncated. Several typos appear.","evidence":[{"where":"End-of-life section","quote":"It acts like a regular sail, utilising a large surface area to"},{"where":"Bibliography","quote":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021_in_spaceflight"},{"where":"Bibliography","quote":"[10 d."},{"where":"Access table","quote":"Feb 2025 11:23:57.461"}]}]}
